= Situated cognition =

'''Situated cognition''' is a family of approaches in cognitive science, psychology, education, philosophy, anthropology, and artificial intelligence that emphasize how thinking and learning depend on the activity, body, environment, tools, social relations, and cultural practices in which they occur. Situated approaches challenge the assumption that cognition can always be explained as context-independent manipulation of internal representations.

The family is heterogeneous. Some situated theories emphasize context-sensitive learning; others emphasize embodiment, action, social interaction, material artifacts, or distributed cognitive systems. Some reject strong internalist or representational accounts, while others retain internal representations but treat them as only one part of a larger cognitive process.<ref name="roth">[https://doi.org/10.1002/wcs.1242 Roth, W.-M. and Jornet, A. (2013), “Situated cognition,” ''WIREs Cognitive Science'' 4(5), 463–478.]</ref>

== Historical background ==

Situated cognition developed through several overlapping traditions.

Lucy Suchman's ''Plans and Situated Actions'' (1987) challenged accounts of action in which people first construct complete internal plans and then execute them mechanically. Her work emphasized how action is organized through ongoing interaction with local circumstances.

Jean Lave's studies of everyday mathematical practice showed that people can solve problems successfully in familiar activities while performing differently on formally similar school tasks.

John Seely Brown, Allan Collins, and Paul Duguid argued that knowledge is partly a product of the activity, context, and culture in which it is learned and used. They proposed cognitive apprenticeship as an educational response.<ref name="brown">[https://doi.org/10.3102/0013189X018001032 Brown, J. S., Collins, A., and Duguid, P. (1989), “Situated Cognition and the Culture of Learning,” ''Educational Researcher'' 18(1), 32–42.]</ref>

Lave and Étienne Wenger later developed the concept of situated learning and legitimate peripheral participation in communities of practice.

== Core claim ==

The weakest situated claim is:

<pre>
context influences cognition
</pre>

This is widely supported.

Stronger claims include:

* knowledge is partly constituted by activity and social practice;
* cognitive processes extend into tools and environments;
* cognition is fundamentally embodied and enacted;
* internal symbolic representation is not always necessary.

The stronger the claim, the more theoretical disagreement exists.

Situated cognition should therefore not be treated as one yes-or-no proposition.

== Context ==

'''Context''' can include:

* physical environment;
* task structure;
* available tools;
* social partners;
* institutional rules;
* language;
* cultural practices;
* prior activity;
* goals.

Context is not merely a list of background variables.

In strong situated accounts, the activity and context partly define the cognitive task itself.

A cashier calculating a price adjustment with a register is not necessarily solving the same cognitive problem as a student answering an abstract arithmetic worksheet.

== Knowing and doing ==

Situated approaches reject a strict separation between abstract knowledge and practical use.

Brown, Collins, and Duguid argued that conventional education often treats knowledge as a self-contained substance that can be learned independently of its use context.<ref name="brown" />

Situated learning instead emphasizes authentic activity.

The learner acquires not only propositions but also:

* procedures;
* interpretive habits;
* social norms;
* tool use;
* task framing.

This does not imply that abstract knowledge is impossible.

It implies that abstraction itself is learned through practices.

== Transfer ==

A central challenge is '''transfer''': whether knowledge learned in one context can be used in another.

Strong context dependence predicts limited transfer.

Classical cognitive theories often expect broader generalization when underlying structure is shared.

Empirical results vary.

Transfer is more likely when learners:

* recognize structural similarity;
* understand principles;
* encounter multiple examples;
* practice abstraction;
* receive cues connecting contexts.

Situated cognition therefore does not require the claim that knowledge never transfers.

== Authentic activity ==

Authentic activities resemble the practices in which knowledge is actually used.

Examples include:

* apprentices diagnosing real equipment;
* scientists analyzing real data;
* students writing for real audiences;
* programmers working with actual repositories.

Authenticity can improve motivation and contextual understanding.

But authentic tasks can also contain irrelevant complexity.

Educational design should therefore balance realism with instructional clarity.

== Cognitive apprenticeship ==

'''Cognitive apprenticeship''' makes expert thinking visible while preserving meaningful activity.

Common elements include:

* modeling;
* coaching;
* scaffolding;
* articulation;
* reflection;
* exploration.

A teacher can demonstrate reasoning, support early performance, and gradually remove assistance.

The method is inspired by apprenticeship but adapted to cognitive skills.

== Communities of practice ==

A '''community of practice''' is a group organized around recurring shared practice.

Newcomers can learn through participation rather than only formal instruction.

Lave and Wenger described legitimate peripheral participation: newcomers begin with limited but meaningful participation and gradually acquire broader competence and identity within the practice.

Not every community produces accurate or ethical knowledge.

Participation explains learning processes, not epistemic truth.

== Embodied cognition ==

[[Embodied cognition]] emphasizes the role of bodily structure, sensorimotor capacities, and bodily state.

It overlaps with situated cognition because bodies are part of the context in which cognition occurs.

The concepts are not identical.

Situated cognition can emphasize social and cultural context without claiming that bodily morphology explains the phenomenon.

== Distributed cognition ==

[[Distributed cognition]] analyzes cognitive processes spread across people, artifacts, and representations.

Edwin Hutchins's work on navigation and other coordinated systems shows how cognitive tasks can be accomplished through structured interaction among participants and tools.

Situated cognition is broader.

Distributed cognition makes a stronger analytical commitment to examining cognitive processes across system boundaries.

== Enactive cognition ==

Enactive approaches emphasize cognition as emerging through active organism-environment coupling.

Perception is treated as something agents do through skilled engagement, not merely passive internal reconstruction.

Enactivism overlaps with situated and embodied cognition while adding specific theoretical commitments about autonomy, sense-making, and representation.

== Affordances ==

An '''affordance''' is an opportunity for action relative to an agent's capabilities.

A chair affords sitting for some bodies.

A button affords pressing for a user who recognizes its function.

Affordances connect environmental structure with action possibilities.

The concept originates in ecological psychology and has been widely used in situated and embodied accounts.

== External representations ==

People think with:

* diagrams;
* written notes;
* maps;
* calculators;
* computer interfaces;
* physical arrangements.

External representations can reduce memory load, make relations visible, and enable operations that would be difficult mentally.

A written multiplication algorithm changes the cognitive task by stabilizing intermediate states.

Tools can therefore be constitutive parts of a task process without implying they literally become parts of the biological brain.

== Indexicality ==

Language often depends on situation.

Words such as:

* here;
* now;
* this;
* I;

cannot be interpreted fully without context.

Brown, Collins, and Duguid used such examples to illustrate how meaning can be context-dependent.<ref name="brown" />

Situated cognition generalizes the lesson beyond language.

== Social cognition ==

Many cognitive tasks depend on interaction.

Conversation, teaching, negotiation, collaborative problem solving, and joint action involve:

* turn-taking;
* shared attention;
* role expectations;
* mutual adjustment.

Individual cognitive models can miss these dynamics if they treat the social environment as static input.

== Culture ==

Cultural practices shape:

* what counts as a problem;
* which tools are available;
* which solutions are valued;
* how expertise is recognized.

Learning a profession therefore includes learning its practices and norms.

This is a stronger claim than saying culture simply supplies information.

== Representations debate ==

Some situated theorists argue that cognition often operates without explicit internal symbolic representations.

Others retain representational explanations.

The empirical question should be task-specific.

A successful reactive controller shows that one task can be solved without a rich internal world model.

It does not prove that humans never use representations.

Likewise, evidence for representation in one task does not make context irrelevant.

== Robotics ==

Situated and embodied principles influenced behavior-based robotics.

A robot can exploit:

* sensorimotor feedback;
* environmental structure;
* reactive control;
* local rules.

This can reduce the need for central planning.

Rodney Brooks's subsumption architecture is a well-known example of this design tradition.

Robotic success provides a proof of engineering possibility, not direct evidence about human cognition.

== Human–computer interaction ==

Situated action is important in interface design.

Users often adapt their actions to:

* interface feedback;
* interruptions;
* unexpected states;
* collaboration;
* physical environment.

A workflow designed around an idealized plan can fail when real activity is opportunistic and adaptive.

User research should therefore observe work in context.

== Experimental evidence ==

Situated hypotheses can be tested by varying context while keeping other factors controlled.

Possible manipulations include:

* tool access;
* social collaboration;
* environmental cues;
* task framing;
* physical embodiment;
* authentic versus abstract setting.

Outcomes can include:

* accuracy;
* speed;
* strategy;
* retention;
* transfer.

A context effect is evidence that context matters.

It is not automatically evidence for every situated-cognition theory.

== Confounds ==

Authentic and decontextualized tasks can differ in:

* familiarity;
* motivation;
* visual cues;
* difficulty;
* social support.

If these variables are not controlled, performance differences cannot be attributed specifically to situated cognition.

Experimental design should separate plausible mechanisms.

== Education ==

Situated educational design can include:

* authentic problems;
* apprenticeship;
* collaborative projects;
* simulations;
* case-based learning;
* reflection across contexts.

The goal should not be to eliminate abstraction.

Learners often need both contextual practice and explicit generalization.

Transfer can be improved by comparing multiple cases and making underlying structure explicit.

== Situated knowledge versus relativism ==

If knowledge is situated, it does not follow that every belief is equally valid.

Context can shape how knowledge is produced while evidence still constrains claims.

A situated analysis can explain why experts use different tools or categories without denying the possibility of better and worse explanations.

== Artificial agents ==

Artificial agents operate in environments.

A situated AI system can use sensors, tools, external memory, and interaction rather than rely only on internal computation.

A language model connected to retrieval, tools, and persistent state is more situated than an isolated text predictor in the functional sense.

This does not establish embodiment, agency, understanding, or consciousness unless those properties are independently defined and demonstrated.

== Relation to behavior ==

[[Behavior]] provides observable action through which situated cognition is often studied.

Context-sensitive behavior can arise from:

* perception;
* learning;
* memory;
* social norms;
* environmental affordances.

Behavioral context dependence does not uniquely identify one cognitive theory.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Situated cognition is best treated as a family of empirically motivated theories about context dependence and organism-environment interaction.

Well-supported claims that cognition is context-sensitive should be separated from stronger claims that cognition necessarily extends beyond the individual or can operate entirely without representation.

